+# LEONIDA — Architecture review
+
+This was a read-through of `src/core`, the system registry, the scene-graph ownership rules and a sample of systems (pickups, peds, vehicles, interiors, player, world). `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` was a listed deliverable in TEAM-PLAN §5 that never got written; this fills the gap. It records what is already good, what was changed in this pass, and what is worth doing next. Nothing here was run in a browser (see "Verification" at the end).
+
+## How it fits together
+
+```
+main → Game (core)
+        ├─ services: Events (typed bus), Input, Time, Physics (Rapier), Save, Perf, Debug
+        ├─ scene roots: worldRoot (city, hidden indoors) · interiorRoot · actorsRoot (always visible)
+        └─ SystemRegistry ← systems discovered via import.meta.glob('../systems/*/index.ts')
+```
+
+- **A system is a folder** with an `index.ts` that default-exports `{ id, priority, create(game) }` and returns a `GameSystem` (`init`, optional `fixedUpdate`, `update`, `serialize`, `deserialize`, `dispose`, `debugActions`). Priority bands are in `src/core/System.ts`.
+- **Systems talk through typed contracts**, not imports of each other: `src/core/peers.ts` defines `IVehiclesPeer`, `IPedsPeer`, `IDamagePeer`, and so on. `Game` exposes them as optional getters (`game.peds?.…`), so any system can be absent. Cross-system *facts* go over the typed event bus (`src/core/Events.ts`).
+- **Fault isolation**: a system that throws in `init`, `update` or `fixedUpdate` is logged, disabled for the session, and the loop keeps running. Isolation sets (`?systems=world,player,camera`) make every system testable alone.
+- **Pure logic is separated from rendering** where it pays off (`src/casino`, road graph, spatial hash, wanted machine) and is unit-tested without a browser.
+
+## What is good (keep it)
+
+1. Contracts-over-imports (`peers.ts` + `Events.ts`) is the right shape for a codebase written by several agents or people in parallel.
+2. Lazy, glob-discovered systems with per-system fault isolation and `debugActions` make the game unusually debuggable (`__game.debug.run(...)`, `&actions=`, `&autotest=`).
+3. Data tables live in `src/data`, per-system tunables in `config.ts` files, so balancing does not touch logic.
+4. Fixed-step physics with an interpolation `alpha` and a capped substep count prevents the death spiral after a hitch.
+5. Instanced renderers with pooled slots (pickups, peds, vehicles, particles) keep draw calls flat.
+
+## Changes made in this pass
+
+| Change | Why |
+|---|---|
+| `Game.frame` / `Game.fixedStep` no longer allocate two closures per system per tick; they use an inline `try/catch` that calls the same `disable` path. | The loop ran ~30 systems at 60 Hz, so this removed up to roughly 3,600 short-lived closures per second. Behaviour is identical: a throwing system is still disabled and reported. |
+| The window `resize` listener is stored and removed in `Game.dispose`. | It was an anonymous arrow that was never removed. |
+| New system `ambient` (seagull flocks), `PRIORITY.ambient = 240`. | "Make the world feel alive" without touching any other system. See [progress/ambient.md](progress/ambient.md). |
+| `docs/PROGRESS.md` §1 and §4 corrected; README note updated. | Four listed "known issues" (invisible in interiors, run-over kills bypassing `damage.apply`, double hospital fee, airborne-save fall) are already fixed in code; the docs still described the pre-fix state. |
+
+## Recommended next steps (not done)
+
+Ordered by value for effort. None were attempted blind because the project's dependencies could not be installed in the review environment, so there was no way to run the tests or the game after a refactor.
+
+1. **Browser QA first.** The README itself says the build has had very little hands-on play-testing, and every autotest is unexecuted. Run `npm install && npm test && npm run smoke`, then run the autotests (`&autotest=<n>`). Fix what fails before adding features.
+2. **Split the files over the ~400-line guideline**: `RoadGraph.ts` (480), `Ped.ts` (473), `peds/index.ts` (433), `Vehicle.ts` (430), `police/index.ts` (420). The seams are visible: ped spawning vs. event wiring in `peds/index.ts`; a physics-body helper out of `Vehicle.ts`.
+3. **Remove the `PedProxy` stopgap in `shops`** once interiors are confirmed to show the real actors.
+4. **Replace the polled `E` key** shared by casino stations and interior exit with an interaction-prompt registry (one system owns "what does E do here", others register prompts). This is the one place where systems implicitly compete for an input.
+5. **Make save/load a tested contract.** Each system implements `serialize`/`deserialize`, but the README notes it has not been exercised end to end. A round-trip test (`serialize → deserialize → serialize` is stable) per system would catch most bugs cheaply, and wanted level and police units are deliberately not saved today.
+6. **Replace string-keyed `perf.set('name', n)` and `debug.run('id.action')`** with typed keys if the system count keeps growing. Typos currently fail silently.
+7. **Bigger-ticket "alive" ideas**, once the above are stable: audible ambience tied to the gull flocks, weather (rain/wind recipes already exist in audio), ped gestures and idle chatter, traffic yielding and overtaking, a garage interior for the chop shop.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `tests/ambient/flock.test.ts` is new (vitest). Its logic was also run outside vitest with `tsx` and `node:assert` against the real source, and every check passed.
+- A scoped `tsc` run over the new ambient files and `Game.ts` / `System.ts` showed no errors attributable to these changes. It could not be a full `npm run typecheck`, because `node_modules` (three, rapier, vite, vitest) was not installable in that environment.
+- Nothing was run in a browser. Treat the seagulls' look (size, speed, altitude, flap) as a first draft to tune in `src/systems/ambient/config.ts`.
