+# ambient
+
+Cosmetic world life that no other system owns. Folder: `src/systems/ambient/`. Priority 240 (after pickups, before weapons). Nothing here is saved and nothing affects gameplay.
+
+## What exists
+
+- **Seagull flocks.** 5 flocks (31 birds) circle just offshore at the beach (east), the port (north) and the docks (south). One `InstancedMesh`, one draw call, parented to `worldRoot` so interiors hide it for free.
+- **Behaviour.** Each bird orbits its flock anchor at its own radius, altitude and speed, banks into the turn, and bobs slightly. By day they glide; the wing flap (Y-scale squash on the instance) is a gentle shimmer.
+- **Reactions.** A `weapon:fired` within 70 m of a flock anchor, or a `vehicle:exploded` within 180 m, scatters it: the orbit widens and lifts, the birds speed up and flap hard, then settle over 7 s.
+- **Day/night.** Birds shrink out between `night01` 0.55 and 0.85 and are hidden at night.
+- **Culling.** No per-frame work when the camera is farther than about 570 m from every flock, or inside an interior.
+
+## Files
+
+| File | Role |
+|---|---|
+| `config.ts` | Flock anchors and every tunable. Change look and feel here. |
+| `flock.ts` | Pure math (orbit, scare easing, night fade). Unit-tested. |
+| `SeagullRenderer.ts` | Gull geometry (body, tail, two wings with darker tips) and the instanced mesh. |
+| `index.ts` | The system: event wiring, culling, per-frame update, debug actions. |
+
+## How to test
+
+- `npm test` runs `tests/ambient/flock.test.ts`.
+- In the browser: `?systems=world,player,camera,ambient`, then `__game.debug.run('ambient.flocks')` and `__game.debug.run('ambient.scare')`. Walk to a shoreline (for example `world.goto` or `player.teleport:925:0`) at midday.
+- `core.perf` reports a `gulls` count while any flock is being drawn.
+
+## Not done / known gaps
+
+- Not yet seen in a browser: size, speed, altitude and flap amplitude are first guesses.
+- No audio (gull calls would fit the existing `audio` recipe system).
+- Flocks do not react to the player running or driving close; only to gunfire and explosions.
+- Anchors are fixed constants, not derived from the shoreline of the generated map.
